          <setvar action="Set" varname="SCORE">100</setvar>
        </respcondition>
      </resprocessing>
      <itemfeedback ident="9217_fb">
        <flow_mat>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/html">In family history research, the paternal line refers to your father's family and the maternal line refers to your mother's family. Tracking each branch separately helps avoid confusion when organizing genealogical data across multiple generations.</mattext>
          </material>
        </flow_mat>
      </itemfeedback>
      <itemfeedback ident="8212_fb">
        <flow_mat>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;p&gt;Neither line is defined by age or generation—both lines include ancestors across all generations. The distinction is not between living and deceased relatives, as both lines include people in both categories. While the paternal line traces through the father and the maternal line through the mother, this does not mean all relatives in each line are the same gender. The defining feature is which parent's family each line descends from.&lt;/p&gt;</mattext>
          </material>
        </flow_mat>
      </itemfeedback>
      <itemfeedback ident="89756_fb">
        <flow_mat>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;p&gt;Neither line is defined by age or generation—both lines include ancestors across all generations. The distinction is not between living and deceased relatives, as both lines include people in both categories. While the paternal line traces through the father and the maternal line through the mother, this does not mean all relatives in each line are the same gender. The defining feature is which parent's family each line descends from.&lt;/p&gt;</mattext>
          </material>
        </flow_mat>
      </itemfeedback>
      <itemfeedback ident="90448_fb">
        <flow_mat>
